_is_analyzed_module shortens the callee module because the slot may carry a
trailing TYPE name -- app.config.Loader for a callee defined in app/config.py.
The shortening was UNBOUNDED: it walked every dotted prefix down to a single
component, so it equally licensed os.path -> os and crypto.tls -> crypto. A repo
owning a directory named `os` therefore reported boundary coverage COMPLETE over
the standard library's os.path, with nothing about os.path examined -- a clean
verdict over an unexamined module, produced by a directory-name collision.

Same shape as INV-juvul's finding one layer up, where folding os/exec to os.exec
and then shortening suppressed the SUBPROCESS module because caddy owns
internal/filesystems/os.go. That was fixed by testing the FOLDED spelling whole.
The dotted loop was left unbounded.

TWO BOUNDS, EACH FOR ITS OWN REASON.
  ONE STEP, because that is the length of a type name. A nested class
    (app.config.Loader.Inner) needs two, is not licensed, and reports a gap --
    the safe direction.
  NEVER TO A SINGLE COMPONENT, because a bare os / crypto / json / time is
    precisely the spelling that collides with an ordinary directory name, and
    the analyzed set is matched as a component-bounded INFIX (it must be, to
    tolerate packaging prefixes), which makes a one-component needle match
    almost anywhere.

NOT "match the shortened form as a SUFFIX instead". That was the first design
and INV-liloh's own fixture refutes it: the src-layout callee
hypergumbo_core.scip._generated shortens to hypergumbo_core.scip, which sits
INSIDE packages.hypergumbo-core.src.hypergumbo_core.scip.loader and is not a
suffix of it. A suffix rule would have re-broken that fix.

